.TH WUW 1 "October 2026" "wuw 1.0.8" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wuw \- show what is happening with a runit or SysV service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wuw
.RB [ \-n " lines" ]
.I service
.RB [ l ]
.br
.B wuw \-\-list-services
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wuw
prints a compact service status containing its enabled and active state,
process tree, resource use, and recent readable log messages.
It uses runit when a service directory is present and otherwise falls back to
the service's SysV init script.
.PP
Service status requires root privileges. When a requested service exists and
.B wuw
is invoked by another user, it re-executes its absolute path through
.BR sudo (8),
preserving the service name, log action and
.B \-n
option. Service listing and shell completion do not invoke
.BR sudo .
.PP
Append
.B l
to browse a per-service log in
.BR less (1)
instead of printing status. The same automatic privilege elevation applies.
The current log opens at its end. Completed svlogd rotations
.RI ( @*.s )
are available newest first: type
.B :n
for an older file and
.B :p
for a newer one. Scrolling stays within the selected file.
Press
.B Shift+F
to follow new entries,
.B Ctrl+C
to stop following, and
.B q
to quit. Following reopens the current file after rotation.
.PP
Files are opened directly without concatenating the history or counting
all lines. Shared syslog files are not scanned in this mode, and timestamps
are displayed as stored. The pager runs in secure mode with shell/editor
commands and input preprocessors disabled.
.SH RUNIT STATE
The Active line reports the current state and Wanted reports the requested
state. A red blinking dot means the service is down and wants to start.
A green blinking dot means it is stopping or restarting. Only the dot blinks;
text stays steady and names the transition even without colours.
ANSI blink support in the terminal is required to see the animation.
.PP
An expected down state is grey, including a completed one-time run.
A running one-time service stays green and reports Wanted: down (no restart).
Runit status does not retain the exit code, so
an expected down state does not certify successful completion.
The service's state, PID and age are read independently of its logger.
.PP
Seconds shown during a transition measure the age of the current runit state,
not the age of the start/stop request, which runit does not expose.
Running means a process is executing; it does not certify readiness.
.SH MEMORY ACCOUNTING
Memory is estimated by summing proportional set size (PSS) and page-table
memory for the main process and its descendants. This avoids counting shared
mappings once for every process. If PSS cannot be read for every process, WUW
falls back to summed resident set size for the complete process tree. The
output is labelled
.B (estimated)
or
.B (RSS)
accordingly. Neither method includes all memory tracked by a dedicated cgroup.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " lines"
Show at most this many recent log lines in status output. The default is 12.
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Display a usage summary.
.TP
.B \-\-list-services
List discoverable runit and SysV service names, one per line. This interface is
used by the packaged shell completions.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WUW_COLOR
Set to
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour in automatic mode when set to a non-empty value.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 when the service is active, 3 when it is inactive or its state is
unknown, 4 when no service definition can be found, and 2 for invalid usage.
Log browsing returns the pager's exit status, or 1 when no readable per-service
log or pager is available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/service
Enabled runit services.
.TP
.I /etc/sv
Runit service definitions.
.TP
.I /etc/init.d
SysV init scripts.
.TP
.I /var/log/runit
Common location for per-service runit logs.
.SH AUTHOR
Jacek Stypuła.
